---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import { SITE } from '../site';
import yotascale from '../assets/about/yotascale-first-day.jpg';
import berlin2017 from '../assets/about/berlin-2017.jpg';
import franzOffice from '../assets/about/relayr-franz-office.jpg';
import team from '../assets/work/workshop-team.jpg';
import franzHardware from '../assets/about/relayr-franz-hardware.jpg';
import covidDesk from '../assets/about/relayr-covid-home-office.jpg';
import deskToday from '../assets/about/desk-today.jpg';

---

{/* Photos sit beside the part of the story they belong to; they stack under the text on narrow screens. */}
<Base
  title="About"
  description="About Syed Usman, an infrastructure engineer and former Solutions Architect in Berlin: background, how he works, and how to get in touch."
>
  <div class="wrap about">
    <header class="page-header narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>About</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        I’m Syed, an infrastructure engineer in Berlin. This is the longer version of how I got here and how I like to
        work.
      </p>
    </header>

    <h2 class="section-label">The short version</h2>

    <section class="story">
      <div class="story-text prose">
        <p>
          I studied software engineering at NUST in Islamabad, graduated as a gold medallist in 2015, and started my
          career at YotaScale, working between Palo Alto and Lahore on a pipeline that dug through AWS bills to find
          waste.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="story-photos">
        <Figure src={yotascale} alt="Syed smiling at his desk in front of two monitors and a laptop" caption="First day at my first job, YotaScale, 2015." />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story story-flip">
      <div class="story-text prose">
        <p>
          In 2017 I moved to Berlin. After a year at Colormass moving a Django monolith onto Kubernetes, I joined relayr,
          Munich Re’s industrial IoT company, and spent five years in industrial IoT, growing from solutions engineer to
          senior solutions engineer to solutions architect. As an architect I was also the tech lead of a team of 8
          engineers, even though it wasn’t in my title.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="story-photos story-photos-pair">
        <Figure src={berlin2017} alt="Syed taking a selfie in front of the Brandenburg Gate on a sunny day" caption="New in Berlin, July 2017." />
        <Figure src={team} alt="Syed with four teammates in front of a retrospective board and a video call screen; teammates’ faces blurred" caption="With my team at relayr, 2020 (teammates blurred)." />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story">
      <div class="story-text prose">
        <p>
          Working with hardware changed how I think about deploys. A bad deploy to a cloud service costs you an hour.
          A bad deploy to a gateway bolted inside a lift shaft costs you a site visit. I learned a lot there about
          vendors, over-the-air updates, and debugging a mess of wires in the office at midnight.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="story-photos story-photos-pair">
        <Figure src={franzOffice} alt="Syed laughing at his laptop in the relayr office, with boxes of gateways and cables on the desk in front" caption="The relayr office during Franz, our lift-monitoring product." />
        <Figure src={franzHardware} alt="A desk covered in computer parts: an open small case, a power supply, a Ryzen CPU box and a screwdriver" caption="Building hardware for Franz." />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="story story-flip">
      <div class="story-text prose">
        <p>
          After that I built an AWS platform at Jillion. Today I’m a Senior DevOps/Infrastructure Engineer at
          <a href="https://www.multiverse.io">Multiverse</a> (I joined StackFuel, which Multiverse acquired in 2025),
          where I run the Kubernetes platform behind hands-on courses for more than 10,000 learners. The full history
          is on <a href="/work/">my work page</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="story-photos story-photos-pair">
        <Figure src={covidDesk} alt="A home office desk by a sunny window, with a laptop on a stand, a monitor, sticky notes and a whiteboard" caption="Working from home in Berlin during covid." />
        <Figure src={deskToday} alt="A standing desk with a monitor and laptop in a bright attic room with two windows" caption="My desk today." />
      </div>
    </section>

    <h2 class="section-label">How I work</h2>
    <div class="narrow prose how-i-work">
      <p>
        When something breaks, I want evidence before opinions. I go layer by layer and write down what I’ve ruled out,
        including my own wrong guesses, so nobody has to check the same thing twice.
      </p>
      <p>
        I don’t consider a fix done until it’s in Terraform, Helm or Ansible. I’ve had a live patch quietly undone by
        the next deploy, and once is enough. The same goes for backups: I only trust them once someone has restored from
        one, so I write the runbook and then we run the drill.
      </p>
      <p>
        I like docs that are short and explain why, not just what. And when AI agents work on our infrastructure, they
        get the same treatment as any service account: their own identity, as little access as possible, and nothing
        they read is trusted.
      </p>
      <p>
        As a tech lead I hired people, ran 1:1s, planned with clients and handled incidents. Mostly, though, the job was
        clearing the way so the team could ship.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="about-closing">
      <section>
        <h2 class="section-label">The work I enjoy most</h2>
        <p>
          Reliability problems that cut across layers, platforms other teams depend on, and teams that write things
          down. I’m happiest somewhere between hands-on engineering and helping a team decide what to build next, as a
          lead or staff engineer, or as an engineering manager who still looks at the dashboards.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2 class="section-label">Languages</h2>
        <p>
          I speak English natively and German at B1, and it’s slowly getting better. I got the AWS Solutions Architect –
          Associate certification in 2018, but most of what I know I learned in production.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2 class="section-label">Get in touch</h2>
        <p>
          The best way to reach me is email: <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>. I’m also on
          <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/syedusmankaleem/">LinkedIn</a> and
          <a href="https://github.com/syed-usman">GitHub</a>.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
